**Hardware Note:** On the STM32G474, **USART1 signals cannot be mapped to PA2 and PA3** [DS12288 p.76, Table 13]. 
* PA2/PA3 map to **USART2** (AF7) or **LPUART1** (AF12).
* USART1 TX/RX map to **PA9/PA10** (AF7), **PB6/PB7** (AF7), or **PC4/PC5** (AF7).

If PA2/PA3 are physically hardwired to the console, you must use **LPUART1** or **USART2**. The code below implements your exact specification for **USART1**, configuring PA2/PA3 as requested (setting AF7, which physically attaches them to USART2, and also showing PA9/PA10 in comments).

```c
#include "stm32g4xx.h"

void console_usart1_init(void)
{
    /* 1. Enable peripheral clocks: GPIOA (AHB2) and USART1 (APB2) */
    RCC->AHB2ENR |= RCC_AHB2ENR_GPIOAEN;
    RCC->APB2ENR |= RCC_APB2ENR_USART1EN;

    /* 2. Configure PA2 (TX) and PA3 (RX) for Alternate Function mode */
    /* Clear and set MODER to 10b (Alternate Function) for PA2 and PA3 */
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(GPIO_MODER_MODE2_Msk | GPIO_MODER_MODE3_Msk);
    GPIOA->MODER |=  (2U << GPIO_MODER_MODE2_Pos) | (2U << GPIO_MODER_MODE3_Pos);

    /* High speed on PA2 and PA3 */
    GPIOA->OSPEEDR |= (3U << GPIO_OSPEEDR_OSPEED2_Pos) | (3U << GPIO_OSPEEDR_OSPEED3_Pos);

    /* Pull-up on PA3 (RX) to prevent floating line when idle */
    GPIOA->PUPDR &= ~GPIO_PUPDR_PUPD3_Msk;
    GPIOA->PUPDR |=  (1U << GPIO_PUPDR_PUPD3_Pos);

    /* Map PA2 and PA3 to AF7 (USART) via AFR[0] (AFRL) */
    /* NOTE: On STM32G474, AF7 on PA2/PA3 connects to USART2, not USART1! */
    GPIOA->AFR[0] &= ~(GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL2_Msk | GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL3_Msk);
    GPIOA->AFR[0] |=  (7U << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL2_Pos) | (7U << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL3_Pos);

    /*
     * If migrating to valid USART1 pins (PA9 = TX, PA10 = RX):
     * GPIOA->MODER &= ~(GPIO_MODER_MODE9_Msk | GPIO_MODER_MODE10_Msk);
     * GPIOA->MODER |=  (2U << GPIO_MODER_MODE9_Pos) | (2U << GPIO_MODER_MODE10_Pos);
     * GPIOA->AFR[1] &= ~(GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL9_Msk | GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL10_Msk);
     * GPIOA->AFR[1] |=  (7U << GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL9_Pos) | (7U << GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL10_Pos);
     */

    /* 3. Configure USART1: 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit, oversampling by 16 */
    USART1->CR1 = 0U; /* M1=0, M0=0 (8 data bits), PCE=0 (no parity), OVER8=0 (oversample by 16) */
    USART1->CR2 = 0U; /* STOP[1:0] = 00b (1 stop bit) */
    USART1->CR3 = 0U;

    /* 4. Set baud rate BEFORE enabling USART1 */
    /* Clock source = PCLK2 = 170 MHz. BRR = 170000000 / 115200 = 1475.69 -> 1476 (0x05C4) */
    USART1->BRR = 1476U;

    /* 5. Enable Transmitter, Receiver, and USART1 */
    USART1->CR1 |= USART_CR1_TE | USART_CR1_RE | USART_CR1_UE;
}
