### Interfacing a 5 V Sensor to an nRF52840 SAADC Input

---

### 1. Hardware Constraints & SAADC Input Range

1. **Absolute Maximum & Operating Ratings**:
   - The recommended operating range for supply voltage $V_{DD}$ is $1.7\text{ V}$ (min), $3.0\text{ V}$ (typ), up to $3.6\text{ V}$ (max) [nRF52840_PS p.971].
   - For any I/O pin, when $V_{DD} \le 3.6\text{ V}$, the absolute maximum rating is $V_{I/O} \le V_{DD} + 0.3\text{ V}$ (and never below $-0.3\text{ V}$ or above $3.9\text{ V}$) [nRF52840_PS p.972].
   - Applying an unattenuated 5.0 V signal directly to an nRF52840 pin will forward-bias the internal ESD protection diodes and damage the device.
   - For analog inputs specifically: *“Inputs AIN0 to AIN7 cannot exceed $V_{DD}$ or be lower than $V_{SS}$”* [nRF52840_PS p.677].

2. **SAADC Reference & Gain**:
   - The nRF52840 SAADC features an internal bandgap reference $V_{\text{REF}} = 0.6\text{ V}$ [nRF52840_PS p.677].
   - Available gain settings include $1/6, 1/5, 1/4, 1/3, 1/2, 1, 2, 4$ (`CH[n].CONFIG.GAIN`) [nRF52840_PS p.697].
   - In single-ended mode with internal reference $0.6\text{ V}$ and gain set to **$1/6$**:
     $$\text{Full-Scale Input Range} = \frac{V_{\text{REF}}}{\text{Gain}} = \frac{0.6\text{ V}}{1/6} = 3.6\text{ V}$$
     *(Note: When $V_{DD} = 3.0\text{ V}$, the pin voltage must never exceed $V_{DD}$, so the usable input range on the pin is $0\text{ V}$ to $3.0\text{ V}$.)*

---

### 2. Resistor Divider Sizing & Acquisition Time ($T_{ACQ}$)

To scale a maximum sensor output of $5.0\text{ V}$ down to a voltage below $V_{DD} = 3.0\text{ V}$:

#### A. Target Ratio & Resistor Selection
A $2:1$ divider ratio ($k_{\text{div}} = 0.5$) scales $5.0\text{ V}$ down to $2.5\text{ V}$:
- At $5.0\text{ V}$ input:
  $$V_{\text{pin}} = 5.0\text{ V} \times \frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2}$$
- Choosing standard 1% resistors:
  - **$R_1 = 10\text{ k}\Omega$** (sensor output to AIN0)
  - **$R_2 = 10\text{ k}\Omega$** (AIN0 to GND)
  $$V_{\text{pin}} = 5.0\text{ V} \times \frac{10\text{ k}\Omega}{10\text{ k}\Omega + 10\text{ k}\Omega} = 2.5\text{ V}$$
  - $2.5\text{ V}$ is below $V_{DD} = 3.0\text{ V}$, preventing pin overvoltage.
  - $2.5\text{ V}$ is within the SAADC single-ended range of $3.6\text{ V}$ (internal reference $0.6\text{ V}$ with Gain $1/6$).

#### B. Thevenin Source Resistance & SAADC Acquisition Time
The equivalent source resistance seen by the ADC pin is:
$$R_{\text{source}} = R_{\text{Th}} = \frac{10\text{ k}\Omega \times 10\text{ k}\Omega}{10\text{ k}\Omega + 10\text{ k}\Omega} = 5.0\text{ k}\Omega$$

According to the nRF52840 Product Specification Table 41 (*Acquisition time*) [nRF52840_PS p.676]:
| $T_{\text{ACQ}}$ | Maximum Source Resistance ($R_{\text{source}}$) |
|---|---|
| **$3\ \mu\text{s}$** | **$10\text{ k}\Omega$** |
| **$5\ \mu\text{s}$** | **$40\text{ k}\Omega$** |
| $10\ \mu\text{s}$ | $100\text{ k}\Omega$ |
| $15\ \mu\text{s}$ | $200\text{ k}\Omega$ |
| $20\ \mu\text{s}$ | $400\text{ k}\Omega$ |
| $40\ \mu\text{s}$ | $800\text{ k}\Omega$ |

- Because $R_{\text{source}} = 5.0\text{ k}\Omega \le 10\text{ k}\Omega$, an acquisition time of **$T_{ACQ} = 3\ \mu\text{s}$** or **$5\ \mu\text{s}$** provides sufficient time to charge the internal sampling capacitance.

---

### 3. Pin Selection
The analog inputs on the nRF52840 correspond to fixed analog functions on Port 0 [nRF52840_PS p.927]:
- `AIN0`: P0.02
- `AIN1`: P0.03
- `AIN2`: P0.04
- `AIN3`: P0.05
- `AIN4`: P0.28
- `AIN5`: P0.29
- `AIN6`: P0.30
- `AIN7`: P0.31

In this configuration, we connect the divider output to **`AIN0` (P0.02)**.

---

### 4. SAADC Channel & Peripheral Register Settings

1. **`CH[0].CONFIG` (Offset `0x518`)**:
   - `RESP = 0` (`Bypass`): Internal resistor ladder bypassed.
   - `RESN = 0` (`Bypass`): Internal resistor ladder bypassed.
   - `GAIN = 0` (`Gain1_6`): $1/6$ gain setting.
   - `REFSEL = 0` (`Internal`): Internal $0.6\text{ V}$ reference.
   - `TACQ = 1` (`5us`): $5\ \mu\text{s}$ acquisition time.
   - `MODE = 0` (`SE`): Single-ended mode.
   - `BURST = 0` (`Disabled`): Standard single conversion.
2. **`CH[0].PSELP` (Offset `0x510`)**:
   - Set to `1` (`AnalogInput0` / AIN0).
3. **`CH[0].PSELN` (Offset `0x514`)**:
   - Set to `0` (`NC`).
4. **`RESOLUTION` (Offset `0x5F0`)**:
   - Set to `2` for **12-bit resolution** ($2^{12} = 4096$).
5. **`RESULT.PTR` & `RESULT.MAXCNT`**:
   - EasyDMA requires a pointer to RAM (`RESULT.PTR`) and sample count (`RESULT.MAXCNT = 1`).

---

### 5. Voltage Conversion Math

In 12-bit single-ended mode ($2^{12} = 4096$), Gain $= 1/6$, and $V_{\text{REF}} = 0.6\text{ V}$:
$$\text{Full-Scale Voltage} = \frac{0.6\text{ V}}{1/6} = 3.6\text{ V} = 3600\text{ mV}$$

The pin voltage in millivolts is:
$$V_{\text{pin}}\text{ [mV]} = \frac{\text{ADC\_Result} \times 3600}{4096}$$

Reconstructing the original sensor voltage across the divider:
$$V_{\text{sensor}}\text{ [mV]} = V_{\text{pin}}\text{ [mV]} \times \frac{R_1 + R_2}{R_2} = V_{\text{pin}}\text{ [mV]} \times 2 = \frac{\text{ADC\_Result} \times 7200}{4096}$$

---

### 6. Firmware Code Example

```c
#include "nrf52840.h"
#include <stdint.h>

static int16_t saadc_sample_buffer[1];

/**
 * @brief Initialize SAADC Channel 0 for single-ended reading of AIN0 (P0.02)
 *        with a 2:1 resistor divider (10k / 10k) on a 0-5V sensor signal.
 */
void saadc_init_channel0_ain0(void)
{
    // Enable SAADC peripheral
    NRF_SAADC->ENABLE = (SAADC_ENABLE_ENABLE_Enabled << SAADC_ENABLE_ENABLE_Pos);

    // Set 12-bit resolution
    NRF_SAADC->RESOLUTION = (SAADC_RESOLUTION_VAL_12bit << SAADC_RESOLUTION_VAL_Pos);

    // Configure Channel 0:
    // - RESP: Bypass internal resistor string
    // - RESN: Bypass internal resistor string
    // - GAIN: 1/6 gain
    // - REFSEL: Internal 0.6 V reference
    // - TACQ: 5 us (well within 40 kOhm limit for 5 us TACQ)
    // - MODE: Single-Ended (SE)
    // - BURST: Disabled
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].CONFIG =
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESP_Bypass     << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESP_Pos)   & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESP_Msk)   |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESN_Bypass     << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESN_Pos)   & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESN_Msk)   |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_GAIN_Gain1_6    << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_GAIN_Pos)   & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_GAIN_Msk)   |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_REFSEL_Internal << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_REFSEL_Pos) & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_REFSEL_Msk) |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_TACQ_5us        << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_TACQ_Pos)   & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_TACQ_Msk)   |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_MODE_SE         << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_MODE_Pos)   & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_MODE_Msk)   |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_BURST_Disabled   << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_BURST_Pos)  & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_BURST_Msk);

    // Connect positive input to AIN0 (P0.02)
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].PSELP = (SAADC_CH_PSELP_PSELP_AnalogInput0 << SAADC_CH_PSELP_PSELP_Pos);

    // Disconnect negative input (SE mode shorts N internally to GND)
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].PSELN = (SAADC_CH_PSELN_PSELN_NC << SAADC_CH_PSELN_PSELN_Pos);

    // Set EasyDMA destination buffer
    NRF_SAADC->RESULT.PTR    = (uint32_t)saadc_sample_buffer;
    NRF_SAADC->RESULT.MAXCNT = 1;
}

/**
 * @brief Trigger a single sample and calculate sensor voltage in millivolts.
 * @return Sensor output voltage in mV (0 to 5000 mV).
 */
uint32_t saadc_read_sensor_mv(void)
{
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STARTED = 0;
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_END     = 0;

    // Start conversion pipeline
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_START = 1;
    while (!NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STARTED);
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STARTED = 0;

    // Trigger sample
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_SAMPLE = 1;
    while (!NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_END);
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_END = 0;

    // Stop SAADC to conserve power
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_STOP = 1;
    while (!NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STOPPED);
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STOPPED = 0;

    int16_t raw = saadc_sample_buffer[0];
    if (raw < 0) {
        raw = 0;
    }

    // Convert raw ADC code to millivolts:
    // Sensor mV = (ADC_code * 3600 mV * 2) / 4096 = (ADC_code * 7200) / 4096
    uint32_t sensor_mv = ((uint32_t)raw * 7200UL) / 4096UL;

    return sensor_mv;
}
```

