### 1. Hardware & Electrical Design

#### 1.1 Voltage Limits & Protection
* **nRF52840 I/O Absolute Maximum**: $V_{I/O} \le V_{DD} + 0.3\text{ V}$ (and must not exceed $3.9\text{ V}$, [nRF52840_PS p. 972]).
* **SAADC Maximum Input Voltage**: The voltage on analog input pins $AIN0\dots AIN7$ must not exceed $V_{DD}$ or drop below $V_{SS}$ during operation ([nRF52840_PS p. 677]).
* For a system running at $V_{DD} = 3.0\text{ V}$ or $3.3\text{ V}$, a $5\text{ V}$ sensor signal directly applied will forward-bias the internal ESD protection diodes, causing current leakage or permanent damage. A voltage divider is required.

---

#### 1.2 Resistor Divider Sizing
To scale a $V_{IN(\max)} = 5.0\text{ V}$ sensor down to a target pin voltage $V_{PIN(\max)} \le 3.0\text{ V}$ (or below $V_{DD}$ and within SAADC dynamic range):

Using the standard internal reference $V_{REF} = 0.6\text{ V}$ with a gain of $1/6$:
$$\text{Full-scale SAADC Input Range} = \frac{V_{REF}}{\text{Gain}} = \frac{0.6\text{ V}}{1/6} = 3.6\text{ V}$$
*(Note: If $V_{DD} < 3.6\text{ V}$, say $3.0\text{ V}$ or $3.3\text{ V}$, the pin voltage must strictly remain $\le V_{DD}$.)*

A clean division factor of **$2:1$** (dividing by 2) gives:
$$V_{PIN} = V_{IN} \cdot \frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2} = 5.0\text{ V} \cdot \frac{100\text{ k}\Omega}{100\text{ k}\Omega + 100\text{ k}\Omega} = 2.50\text{ V}$$
* $2.50\text{ V}$ is comfortably below $V_{DD} = 3.0\text{ V}$ or $3.3\text{ V}$.
* $2.50\text{ V}$ is also within the $3.6\text{ V}$ input range when using $V_{REF} = 0.6\text{ V}$ and Gain = $1/6$.

```
Sensor Output (0 - 5V)
        |
       [R1] = 100 kΩ (1%)
        |
        +-----> AIN0 (P0.02)  [0 - 2.5V]
        |
       [R2] = 100 kΩ (1%)
        |
       GND
```

---

#### 1.3 Source Resistance & Acquisition Time ($T_{ACQ}$)
During sampling, the SAADC connects an internal sampling capacitor to the pin ([nRF52840_PS p. 676]). The Thevenin equivalent source impedance looking into the divider node is:
$$R_{\text{source}} = R_1 \parallel R_2 = \frac{100\text{ k}\Omega \cdot 100\text{ k}\Omega}{100\text{ k}\Omega + 100\text{ k}\Omega} = 50\text{ k}\Omega$$

According to **nRF52840 PS Table 41 (Acquisition time)** [p. 676]:
| $T_{ACQ}$ | Maximum Source Resistance ($R_{\text{source}}$) |
|---|---|
| $3\,\mu\text{s}$ | $10\text{ k}\Omega$ |
| $5\,\mu\text{s}$ | $40\text{ k}\Omega$ |
| $10\,\mu\text{s}$ | $100\text{ k}\Omega$ |
| $15\,\mu\text{s}$ | $200\text{ k}\Omega$ |
| $20\,\mu\text{s}$ | $400\text{ k}\Omega$ |
| $40\,\mu\text{s}$ | $800\text{ k}\Omega$ |

Since $R_{\text{source}} = 50\text{ k}\Omega > 40\text{ k}\Omega$, select **$T_{ACQ} = 10\,\mu\text{s}$** (`SAADC_CH_CONFIG_TACQ_10us`).
*(Optional: If high-speed sampling or shorter $T_{ACQ}$ is desired, use smaller resistors such as $R_1 = R_2 = 10\text{ k}\Omega \implies R_{\text{source}} = 5\text{ k}\Omega$, which allows $T_{ACQ} = 3\,\mu\text{s}$ at the cost of higher quiescent current.)*

---

### 2. SAADC Channel Configuration

For reading this channel on **AIN0** (pin `P0.02`):
1. **Mode**: Single-ended (`MODE = SE`)
2. **Reference**: Internal $0.6\text{ V}$ reference (`REFSEL = Internal`)
3. **Gain**: $1/6$ (`GAIN = Gain1_6`)
4. **Acquisition Time**: $10\,\mu\text{s}$ (`TACQ = 10us`)
5. **Resistor Ladder**: Bypassed (`RESP = Bypass`, `RESN = Bypass`)
6. **Resolution**: 12-bit (`RESOLUTION = 12bit`)

#### Conversion Math (12-bit Single-Ended)
From [nRF52840_PS §6.23.3, p. 679]:
$$\text{RESULT} = V(P) \cdot \frac{\text{GAIN}}{V_{REF}} \cdot 2^{(\text{RESOLUTION} - m)}$$
For single-ended mode, $m = 0$. With 12-bit resolution:
$$\text{RESULT} = V_{PIN} \cdot \frac{1/6}{0.6\text{ V}} \cdot 2^{12} = V_{PIN} \cdot \frac{4096}{3.6\text{ V}}$$

To calculate the original sensor voltage $V_{IN}$ in millivolts:
$$V_{PIN}\ (\text{mV}) = \frac{\text{RESULT} \cdot 3600\text{ mV}}{4096}$$
$$V_{IN}\ (\text{mV}) = V_{PIN} \cdot \frac{R_1 + R_2}{R_2} = V_{PIN} \cdot 2 = \frac{\text{RESULT} \cdot 7200}{4096}$$

---

### 3. Firmware Implementation

#### Option A: Bare-Metal / CMSIS Register Level
```c
#include "nrf.h"

static int16_t saadc_sample_buffer[1];

void saadc_init(void)
{
    // 1. Set resolution to 12-bit
    NRF_SAADC->RESOLUTION = SAADC_RESOLUTION_VAL_12bit;

    // 2. Configure Channel 0:
    //    - RESP = Bypass (no internal pull-up/down)
    //    - RESN = Bypass
    //    - GAIN = 1/6 (input dynamic range = 0.6V / (1/6) = 3.6V)
    //    - REFSEL = Internal 0.6V
    //    - TACQ = 10 us (covers source resistance up to 100 kΩ)
    //    - MODE = Single-Ended
    //    - BURST = Disabled
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].CONFIG = 
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESP_Bypass   << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESP_Pos)   |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESN_Bypass   << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESN_Pos)   |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_GAIN_Gain1_6  << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_GAIN_Pos)   |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_REFSEL_Internal << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_REFSEL_Pos) |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_TACQ_10us     << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_TACQ_Pos)   |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_MODE_SE       << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_MODE_Pos)   |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_BURST_Disabled << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_BURST_Pos);

    // 3. Connect Channel 0 positive input to AIN0 (P0.02)
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].PSELP = SAADC_CH_PSELP_PSELP_AnalogInput0;
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].PSELN = SAADC_CH_PSELN_PSELN_NC;

    // 4. Configure EasyDMA result buffer
    NRF_SAADC->RESULT.PTR = (uint32_t)saadc_sample_buffer;
    NRF_SAADC->RESULT.MAXCNT = 1;

    // 5. Enable SAADC
    NRF_SAADC->ENABLE = SAADC_ENABLE_ENABLE_Enabled;
}

uint16_t saadc_read_sensor_mv(void)
{
    // Start SAADC and prepare DMA buffer
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STARTED = 0;
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_START = 1;
    while (NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STARTED == 0);

    // Trigger sample
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_END = 0;
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_SAMPLE = 1;
    while (NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_END == 0);

    // Stop SAADC
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STOPPED = 0;
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_STOP = 1;
    while (NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STOPPED == 0);

    int16_t raw = saadc_sample_buffer[0];
    if (raw < 0) raw = 0;

    // Math:
    // V_pin (mV) = (raw * 3600) / 4096
    // V_in  (mV) = V_pin * ((100k + 100k) / 100k) = V_pin * 2
    // V_in  (mV) = (raw * 7200) / 4096
    uint32_t vin_mv = ((uint32_t)raw * 7200UL) / 4096UL;
    return (uint16_t)vin_mv;
}
```

---

#### Option B: Using Nordic nrfx Driver (`nrfx_saadc`)
```c
#include "nrfx_saadc.h"

#define SAADC_CHANNEL_AIN0 0

void saadc_init_nrfx(void)
{
    nrfx_saadc_config_t saadc_config = NRFX_SAADC_DEFAULT_CONFIG;
    saadc_config.resolution = NRF_SAADC_RESOLUTION_12BIT;
    nrfx_saadc_init(&saadc_config, NULL); // Blocking mode

    nrf_saadc_channel_config_t channel_config = {
        .resistor_p = NRF_SAADC_RESISTOR_DISABLED,
        .resistor_n = NRF_SAADC_RESISTOR_DISABLED,
        .gain       = NRF_SAADC_GAIN1_6,
        .reference  = NRF_SAADC_REFERENCE_INTERNAL,
        .acq_time   = NRF_SAADC_ACQTIME_10US,
        .mode       = NRF_SAADC_MODE_SINGLE_ENDED,
        .burst      = NRF_SAADC_BURST_DISABLED,
        .pin_p      = NRF_SAADC_INPUT_AIN0,
        .pin_n      = NRF_SAADC_INPUT_DISABLED
    };

    nrfx_saadc_channel_init(SAADC_CHANNEL_AIN0, &channel_config);
}

int16_t saadc_read_sensor_mv_nrfx(void)
{
    nrf_saadc_value_t raw_val = 0;
    nrfx_saadc_sample_convert(SAADC_CHANNEL_AIN0, &raw_val);
    if (raw_val < 0) raw_val = 0;

    // Scale back: divider ratio 2:1, range 0..3600mV across 4096 counts
    int32_t vin_mv = ((int32_t)raw_val * 7200L) / 4096L;
    return (int16_t)vin_mv;
}
```

