# Reading a 5 V Sensor on an nRF52840 SAADC Input

---

### 1. Electrical Constraints & SAADC Input Limits

1. **Maximum Pin Voltage Limit:**
   - According to the *nRF52840 Product Specification* (Table 101, Absolute Maximum Ratings [nRF52840_PS p.972]), the maximum input voltage on any GPIO/AIN pin when $V_{DD} \le 3.6\text{ V}$ is:
     $$V_{I/O} = V_{DD} + 0.3\text{ V}$$
     (with absolute maximum not exceeding 3.9 V [nRF52840_PS p.972]).
   - The recommended operating range for supply voltage $V_{DD}$ is 1.7 V minimum, 3.0 V typical, and 3.6 V maximum [nRF52840_PS p.971].
   - Direct connection of a 5 V sensor will permanently damage the SoC.

2. **SAADC Reference & Full-Scale Input Range:**
   - The SAADC provides an internal reference: $V_{\text{REF}} = 0.6\text{ V}$ [nRF52840_PS p.677].
   - Gain choices in register `CH[n].CONFIG` include $1/6, 1/4, 1/3, 1/2, 1, 2, 4$ [nRF52840_PS p.697].
   - Selecting **Internal 0.6 V Reference** and **Gain = 1/6** yields an effective single-ended full-scale range:
     $$\text{Input Range} = \frac{0.6\text{ V}}{1/6} = 3.6\text{ V} \quad [nRF52840_PS p.677]$$
   - Inputs AIN0 to AIN7 cannot exceed $V_{DD}$ or be lower than $V_{SS}$ [nRF52840_PS p.677]. To ensure compatibility with standard $V_{DD} = 3.0\text{ V}$ supplies [nRF52840_PS p.971], the voltage divider is sized to scale a 5.0 V sensor output down to 3.0 V.

---

### 2. Resistor Divider Design

#### Schematic:
```text
  5 V Sensor Output (0 - 5.0 V)
         │
         ┌───┐
         │R1 │ (20 kΩ)
         └───┘
           │
           ├────────────► nRF52840 AIN0 pin (P0.02)
           │             
         ┌───┐
         │R2 │ (30 kΩ)
         └───┘
           │
          GND
```

#### Sizing the Resistors:
- **Voltage Division Ratio ($K$):**
  $$K = \frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2} = \frac{30\text{ k}\Omega}{20\text{ k}\Omega + 30\text{ k}\Omega} = 0.6$$
  - At maximum sensor output $V_{\text{sensor}} = 5.0\text{ V}$:
    $$V_{\text{AIN}} = 5.0\text{ V} \times 0.6 = 3.0\text{ V}$$
  - $3.0\text{ V} \le V_{DD}$ (typical $V_{DD} = 3.0\text{ V}$ [nRF52840_PS p.971]) and is within the 3.6 V SAADC input range for Gain = 1/6 [nRF52840_PS p.677].

#### Source Resistance & Acquisition Time:
The equivalent source resistance seen by the SAADC is the parallel combination of $R_1$ and $R_2$:
$$R_{\text{source}} = \frac{R_1 \times R_2}{R_1 + R_2} = \frac{20\text{ k}\Omega \times 30\text{ k}\Omega}{20\text{ k}\Omega + 30\text{ k}\Omega} = 12\text{ k}\Omega$$

According to the *nRF52840 Product Specification* (Table 41: Acquisition time [nRF52840_PS p.676]):
- $t_{\text{ACQ}} = 3\text{ \mu s}$ supports maximum source resistance of $10\text{ k}\Omega$ [nRF52840_PS p.676].
- $t_{\text{ACQ}} = 5\text{ \mu s}$ supports maximum source resistance of $40\text{ k}\Omega$ [nRF52840_PS p.676].
- $t_{\text{ACQ}} = 10\text{ \mu s}$ supports maximum source resistance of $100\text{ k}\Omega$ [nRF52840_PS p.676].

Because $R_{\text{source}} = 12\text{ k}\Omega > 10\text{ k}\Omega$, setting $t_{\text{ACQ}} \ge 5\text{ \mu s}$ (such as $10\text{ \mu s}$ in `CH[0].CONFIG.TACQ` [nRF52840_PS p.698]) provides sufficient settling time for the internal sample capacitance ($C_{\text{SAMPLE}} = 2.5\text{ pF}$ [nRF52840_PS p.715]).

---

### 3. Conversion Formula

For 12-bit single-ended SAADC conversion ($m = 0$ [nRF52840_PS p.679]):
$$\text{RESULT} = V_{\text{AIN}} \times \left(\frac{\text{GAIN}}{\text{REFERENCE}}\right) \times 2^{12} = V_{\text{AIN}} \times \left(\frac{1/6}{0.6\text{ V}}\right) \times 4096 = V_{\text{AIN}} \times \frac{4096}{3.6\text{ V}} \quad [nRF52840_PS p.679]$$

Since $V_{\text{AIN}} = V_{\text{sensor}} \times 0.6$:
$$\text{RESULT} = (V_{\text{sensor}} \times 0.6) \times \frac{4096}{3.6\text{ V}} = V_{\text{sensor}} \times \frac{4096}{3.6\text{ V} / 0.6}$$

Since $3600\text{ mV} / 0.6 = 6000\text{ mV}$, solving for sensor voltage in millivolts yields:
$$V_{\text{sensor}}\text{ (mV)} = \frac{\text{RESULT} \times 6000\text{ mV}}{4096}$$

---

### 4. SAADC Channel Configuration Code

Below is bare-metal C code configuring SAADC Channel 0 on `AIN0` (`P0.02` [nRF52840_PS p.927]):

```c
#include "nrf.h"
#include <stdint.h>

// RAM buffer for EasyDMA [nRF52840_PS p.679]
static volatile int16_t saadc_buffer[1];

void saadc_init_divider_channel(void)
{
    // Enable SAADC [nRF52840_PS p.696]
    NRF_SAADC->ENABLE = (SAADC_ENABLE_ENABLE_Enabled << SAADC_ENABLE_ENABLE_Pos);

    // Set 12-bit resolution [nRF52840_PS p.712]
    NRF_SAADC->RESOLUTION = (SAADC_RESOLUTION_VAL_12bit << SAADC_RESOLUTION_VAL_Pos);

    // Channel 0 configuration [nRF52840_PS p.697, p.698]:
    // - RESP / RESN: Bypass internal resistor ladder
    // - GAIN: 1/6 (Input range = 0.6 V / (1/6) = 3.6 V)
    // - REFSEL: Internal reference (0.6 V)
    // - TACQ: 10 us (covers Rsource up to 100 kOhm; divider Rsource = 12 kOhm)
    // - MODE: Single-ended
    // - BURST: Disabled
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].CONFIG = 
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESP_Bypass     << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESP_Pos)   & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESP_Msk)   |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESN_Bypass     << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESN_Pos)   & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESN_Msk)   |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_GAIN_Gain1_6    << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_GAIN_Pos)   & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_GAIN_Msk)   |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_REFSEL_Internal << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_REFSEL_Pos) & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_REFSEL_Msk) |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_TACQ_10us       << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_TACQ_Pos)   & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_TACQ_Msk)   |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_MODE_SE         << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_MODE_Pos)   & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_MODE_Msk)   |
        ((SAADC_CH_CONFIG_BURST_Disabled  << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_BURST_Pos)  & SAADC_CH_CONFIG_BURST_Msk);

    // Route positive input to AIN0 (pin P0.02) [nRF52840_PS p.698, p.927]
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].PSELP = (SAADC_CH_PSELP_PSELP_AnalogInput0 << SAADC_CH_PSELP_PSELP_Pos);
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].PSELN = (SAADC_CH_PSELN_PSELN_NC << SAADC_CH_PSELN_PSELN_Pos);

    // Calibrate offset once on startup [nRF52840_PS p.681]
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_CALIBRATEDONE = 0;
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_CALIBRATEOFFSET = 1;
    while (!NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_CALIBRATEDONE);
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_CALIBRATEDONE = 0;
}

uint16_t read_5v_sensor_millivolts(void)
{
    // Configure EasyDMA result buffer [nRF52840_PS p.679, p.713, p.714]
    NRF_SAADC->RESULT.PTR    = (uint32_t)saadc_buffer;
    NRF_SAADC->RESULT.MAXCNT = 1;

    // Start SAADC and wait for STARTED event
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STARTED = 0;
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_START = 1;
    while (!NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STARTED);

    // Take one sample and wait for END event
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_END = 0;
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_SAMPLE = 1;
    while (!NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_END);

    // Stop SAADC
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STOPPED = 0;
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_STOP = 1;
    while (!NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STOPPED);

    int16_t raw = saadc_buffer[0];
    if (raw < 0) {
        raw = 0;
    }

    // Convert raw reading to sensor voltage: (raw * 6000 mV) / 4096
    uint32_t sensor_mv = ((uint32_t)raw * 6000UL + 2048UL) / 4096UL;
    return (uint16_t)sensor_mv;
}
```

---

### 5. Summary Checklist
- **Divider:** $R_1 = 20\text{ k}\Omega$, $R_2 = 30\text{ k}\Omega$, scaling 0–5.0 V down to 0–3.0 V ($K = 0.6$).
- **Reference & Gain:** Internal 0.6 V reference with Gain 1/6 ($V_{\text{FS}} = 3.6\text{ V}$ [nRF52840_PS p.677]).
- **Acquisition Time:** For $R_{\text{source}} = 12\text{ k}\Omega$, set $t_{\text{ACQ}} = 10\text{ \mu s}$ (which covers up to $100\text{ k}\Omega$ [nRF52840_PS p.676]).
- **Pin Assignment:** AIN0 is mapped to pin `P0.02` [nRF52840_PS p.927]. Single-ended input requires `PSELN = NC` [nRF52840_PS p.698].

