### Reading a 5 V Sensor on nRF52840 SAADC Using a Resistor Divider

---

### 1. Electrical Constraints & SAADC Voltage Range

1. **Absolute Maximum & Pin Ratings**:
   - The nRF52840 analog inputs (`AIN0`–`AIN7`) must never exceed $V_{\mathrm{DD}} + 0.3\,\text{V}$ (absolute maximum [nRF52840_PS p.972]). In normal operation, input voltages must stay between $0\,\text{V}$ and $V_{\mathrm{DD}}$ [nRF52840_PS p.677].
   - If $V_{\mathrm{DD}} = 3.3\,\text{V}$, driving $5\,\text{V}$ directly into an analog pin would forward-bias the ESD protection diodes and cause overvoltage damage.

2. **SAADC Reference and Full-Scale Range**:
   - The SAADC supports an internal reference of $V_{\mathrm{REF}} = 0.6\,\text{V}$ and gain settings from $1/6$ to $4$ [nRF52840_PS p.677].
   - With $V_{\mathrm{REF}} = 0.6\,\text{V}$ and $\mathrm{GAIN} = 1/6$:
     $$\text{Full-Scale Range} = \frac{V_{\mathrm{REF}}}{\mathrm{GAIN}} = \frac{0.6\,\text{V}}{1/6} = 3.6\,\text{V}$$
   - When $V_{\mathrm{DD}} = 3.3\,\text{V}$, any analog input below $3.3\,\text{V}$ falls safely within the $3.6\,\text{V}$ full-scale range and does not clip.

---

### 2. Resistor Divider Sizing & Acquisition Time

To safely step down a $0\,\text{V}$–$5.0\,\text{V}$ signal to the SAADC input:

```
Sensor (0 - 5 V)
       |
      [R1]
       |
       +-----> nRF52840 AIN0 (P0.02)
       |
      [R2]
       |
      GND
```

#### Selection of $R_1$ and $R_2$
- Choosing equal standard resistors $R_1 = 100\,\text{k}\Omega$ and $R_2 = 100\,\text{k}\Omega$:
  - Division ratio:
    $$\frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2} = \frac{100\,\text{k}\Omega}{100\,\text{k}\Omega + 100\,\text{k}\Omega} = 0.5$$
  - Maximum pin voltage at $5.0\,\text{V}$ sensor output:
    $$5.0\,\text{V} \times 0.5 = 2.5\,\text{V}$$
    $2.5\,\text{V}$ is well below $V_{\mathrm{DD}} = 3.3\,\text{V}$ and below the $3.6\,\text{V}$ SAADC range.
  - Quiescent current drawn from the sensor:
    $$5.0\,\text{V} / (100\,\text{k}\Omega + 100\,\text{k}\Omega) = 5.0\,\text{V} / 200\,\text{k}\Omega = 25\,\mu\text{A}$$

#### Source Resistance & Acquisition Time ($T_{\mathrm{ACQ}}$)
The Thevenin equivalent source resistance seen by the SAADC analog input pin is the parallel combination of $R_1$ and $R_2$:
$$R_{\mathrm{source}} = \frac{R_1 \times R_2}{R_1 + R_2} = \frac{100\,\text{k}\Omega \times 100\,\text{k}\Omega}{100\,\text{k}\Omega + 100\,\text{k}\Omega} = 50\,\text{k}\Omega$$

According to Table 41 (*Acquisition time*, nRF52840 Product Specification p.676):
- $3\,\mu\text{s}$: Maximum source resistance $10\,\text{k}\Omega$
- $5\,\mu\text{s}$: Maximum source resistance $40\,\text{k}\Omega$
- **$10\,\mu\text{s}$**: Maximum source resistance **$100\,\text{k}\Omega$**
- $15\,\mu\text{s}$: Maximum source resistance $200\,\text{k}\Omega$
- $20\,\mu\text{s}$: Maximum source resistance $400\,\text{k}\Omega$
- $40\,\mu\text{s}$: Maximum source resistance $800\,\text{k}\Omega$

Since $R_{\mathrm{source}} = 50\,\text{k}\Omega$ exceeds $40\,\text{k}\Omega$ but is below $100\,\text{k}\Omega$, the acquisition time must be configured to at least **$10\,\mu\text{s}$** (`CH[0].CONFIG.TACQ = 10us`) to allow the internal sampling capacitor ($C_{\mathrm{SAMPLE}} = 2.5\,\text{pF}$) to fully charge.

---

### 3. Voltage Reconstruction Formula

Per Section 6.23.3 (*Digital output*, nRF52840 Product Specification p.679), for single-ended mode ($m = 0$):
$$\mathrm{RESULT} = V_{\mathrm{pin}} \times \left(\frac{\mathrm{GAIN}}{V_{\mathrm{REF}}}\right) \times 2^{\mathrm{RESOLUTION}}$$

For 12-bit resolution ($2^{12} = 4096$), $\mathrm{GAIN} = 1/6$, and $V_{\mathrm{REF}} = 0.6\,\text{V}$:
$$\mathrm{RESULT} = V_{\mathrm{pin}} \times \frac{1/6}{0.6\,\text{V}} \times 4096 = V_{\mathrm{pin}} \times \frac{4096}{3.6\,\text{V}}$$

Solving for $V_{\mathrm{pin}}$:
$$V_{\mathrm{pin}} = \frac{\mathrm{RESULT} \times 3600\,\text{mV}}{4096}$$

Accounting for the voltage divider:
$$V_{\mathrm{sensor}} = V_{\mathrm{pin}} \times \frac{R_1 + R_2}{R_2} = V_{\mathrm{pin}} \times 2$$

---

### 4. SAADC Register Configuration & Bare-Metal Driver

The following bare-metal C implementation configures SAADC Channel 0 on `AIN0` (`P0.02`), samples using EasyDMA, and converts the code back to the original sensor voltage in millivolts.

```c
#include "nrf52840.h"
#include <stdint.h>

#define R1_OHMS             100000UL
#define R2_OHMS             100000UL

// EasyDMA buffer: must reside in RAM
static int16_t saadc_sample_buffer[1];

/**
 * @brief Initialize SAADC Channel 0 for single-ended sampling on AIN0 (P0.02).
 */
void saadc_channel0_init(void)
{
    // 1. Disable SAADC while configuring registers
    NRF_SAADC->ENABLE = (SAADC_ENABLE_ENABLE_Disabled << SAADC_ENABLE_ENABLE_Pos);

    // 2. Configure 12-bit resolution (values 0..4095)
    NRF_SAADC->RESOLUTION = (SAADC_RESOLUTION_VAL_12bit << SAADC_RESOLUTION_VAL_Pos);

    // 3. Select AIN0 (P0.02) as positive analog input
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].PSELP = (SAADC_CH_PSELP_PSELP_AnalogInput0 << SAADC_CH_PSELP_PSELP_Pos);

    // 4. Disconnect negative analog input (single-ended mode)
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].PSELN = (SAADC_CH_PSELN_PSELN_NC << SAADC_CH_PSELN_PSELN_Pos);

    // 5. Configure Channel 0 parameters:
    //    - RESP: Bypass (no pull resistor)
    //    - RESN: Bypass
    //    - GAIN: 1/6 (input range up to 0.6 V / (1/6) = 3.6 V)
    //    - REFSEL: Internal 0.6 V reference
    //    - TACQ: 10 us (accommodates R_source = 50 kOhm <= 100 kOhm)
    //    - MODE: Single-ended (PSELN ignored, internal negative input connected to GND)
    //    - BURST: Disabled
    NRF_SAADC->CH[0].CONFIG = 
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESP_Bypass     << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESP_Pos)   |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESN_Bypass     << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_RESN_Pos)   |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_GAIN_Gain1_6    << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_GAIN_Pos)   |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_REFSEL_Internal << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_REFSEL_Pos) |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_TACQ_10us       << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_TACQ_Pos)   |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_MODE_SE         << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_MODE_Pos)   |
        (SAADC_CH_CONFIG_BURST_Disabled  << SAADC_CH_CONFIG_BURST_Pos);

    // 6. Set EasyDMA buffer pointer and maximum count (1 sample)
    NRF_SAADC->RESULT.PTR    = (uint32_t)saadc_sample_buffer;
    NRF_SAADC->RESULT.MAXCNT = 1;

    // 7. Enable SAADC
    NRF_SAADC->ENABLE = (SAADC_ENABLE_ENABLE_Enabled << SAADC_ENABLE_ENABLE_Pos);
}

/**
 * @brief Perform a single conversion and compute the sensor input voltage.
 * @return Sensor voltage in millivolts (0 to 5000 mV).
 */
uint32_t saadc_read_sensor_mv(void)
{
    // Clear event flags
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STARTED = 0;
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_END     = 0;

    // Trigger START task to set up EasyDMA
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_START = 1;
    while (!NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STARTED);
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STARTED = 0;

    // Trigger SAMPLE task to take a reading
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_SAMPLE = 1;
    while (!NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_END);
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_END = 0;

    // Stop SAADC after acquisition
    NRF_SAADC->TASKS_STOP = 1;
    while (!NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STOPPED);
    NRF_SAADC->EVENTS_STOPPED = 0;

    // Clamp negative values due to slight zero-offset in single-ended mode
    int16_t raw = saadc_sample_buffer[0];
    if (raw < 0) {
        raw = 0;
    }

    // Convert raw 12-bit ADC code to pin voltage (in mV)
    // V_pin = raw * 3600 mV / 4096
    uint32_t v_pin_mv = ((uint32_t)raw * 3600UL) / 4096UL;

    // Scale back up using the resistor divider ratio: (R1 + R2) / R2
    uint32_t v_sensor_mv = (v_pin_mv * (R1_OHMS + R2_OHMS)) / R2_OHMS;

    return v_sensor_mv;
}
```

