### End-to-End Design Plan for STM32G474 Data-Acquisition Node

#### 1. Clock Tree Configuration (HSE to 170 MHz SYSCLK)
- **HSE Input**: 12 MHz crystal oscillator connected to OSC_IN / OSC_OUT.
- **Main PLL Configuration**:
  - Main PLL division factor $M = 3$, giving PLL input frequency $12\,\text{MHz} / 3 = 4\,\text{MHz}$ (valid within the allowable range $2.66\,\text{MHz}$ to $16\,\text{MHz}$ [STM32G474_Datasheet p.123]).
  - PLL multiplication factor $N = 85$, producing VCO frequency $4\,\text{MHz} \times 85 = 340\,\text{MHz}$ (valid within the Range 1 VCO range $96\,\text{MHz}$ to $344\,\text{MHz}$ [STM32G474_Datasheet p.123]).
  - PLL output division factor $R = 2$, yielding $f_{\text{PLL\_R}} = 340\,\text{MHz} / 2 = 170\,\text{MHz}$ (exact target, 0 % frequency error).
- **Core Voltage and Flash Latency**:
  - Operating at $170\,\text{MHz}$ requires Range 1 Boost Mode (`PWR_CR1.VOS = 1`, `PWR_CR5.R1MODE = 0`).
  - Flash wait states: For $HCLK = 170\,\text{MHz}$ in Range 1 Boost Mode, flash latency must be configured to 4 wait states (`FLASH_ACR.LATENCY = 4` [STM32G474_RM0440 p.98]).
- **Bus Prescalers**: AHB Prescaler = 1 ($HCLK = 170\,\text{MHz}$), APB1 Prescaler = 1 ($PCLK1 = 170\,\text{MHz}$), APB2 Prescaler = 1 ($PCLK2 = 170\,\text{MHz}$).

---

#### 2. Pin Selection & Alternate Functions
- **USART2 Pins**:
  - `PA2`: USART2_TX (AF7, Push-Pull, High Speed) [STM32G474_Datasheet p.73].
  - `PA3`: USART2_RX (AF7, Floating Input) [STM32G474_Datasheet p.73].
- **I2C1 Pins**:
  - `PB8`: I2C1_SCL (AF4, Open-Drain) [STM32G474_Datasheet p.74].
  - `PB9`: I2C1_SDA (AF4, Open-Drain) [STM32G474_Datasheet p.74].

---

#### 3. Logic-Level Compatibility Analysis
- Both the STM32G474 and ESP32 operate at $V_{DD} = 3.3\,\text{V}$.
- **STM32 to ESP32 (USART2_TX on PA2 $\to$ ESP32 RX)**:
  - High-level margin: STM32 $V_{OH,\min} = 2.9\,\text{V} \ge \text{ESP32 } V_{IH,\min} = 2.475\,\text{V}$ (PASS) [STM32G474_Datasheet p.128, ESP32_Datasheet p.52].
  - Low-level margin: STM32 $V_{OL,\max} = 0.4\,\text{V} \le \text{ESP32 } V_{IL,\max} = 0.825\,\text{V}$ (PASS) [STM32G474_Datasheet p.130, ESP32_Datasheet p.52].
  - Overvoltage check: STM32 drives up to $3.3\,\text{V} \le 3.6\,\text{V}$ max input rating of ESP32 (PASS).
- **ESP32 to STM32 (ESP32 TX $\to$ USART2_RX on PA3)**:
  - High-level margin: ESP32 $V_{OH,\min} = 2.64\,\text{V} \ge \text{STM32 } V_{IH,\min} = 2.31\,\text{V}$ (PASS) [ESP32_Datasheet p.52, STM32G474_Datasheet p.128].
  - Low-level margin: ESP32 $V_{OL,\max} = 0.33\,\text{V} \le \text{STM32 } V_{IL,\max} = 0.99\,\text{V}$ (PASS) [ESP32_Datasheet p.52, STM32G474_Datasheet p.128].
  - Input tolerance: PA3 is a 5 V-tolerant (FT) I/O pin rated up to $5.5\,\text{V}$ / $3.6\,\text{V}$ [STM32G474_Datasheet p.84].
- **I2C1 Bus**: Open-drain architecture with external $4.7\,\text{k}\Omega$ pull-ups to $3.3\,\text{V}$ provides safe bidirectional communication.

---

#### 4. Peripheral Baud Rate & Timing Calculations
- **USART2 Baud Rate Register (BRR)**:
  - Peripheral clock: $f_{PCLK1} = 170\,\text{MHz}$.
  - Target baud: $921600\,\text{baud}$, oversampling by 16 (`OVER8 = 0`).
  - Calculation: $\text{BRR} = 170000000 / 921600 \approx 184.46 \implies \text{BRR} = 184$.
  - Resulting baud rate: $170000000 / 184 \approx 923913\,\text{baud}$.
  - Error: $(923913 - 921600) / 921600 \times 100\% = 0.034\% \approx 0.03\%$, well within the $\pm 2\%$ tolerance requirement.
- **I2C1 Timing Register (TIMINGR)**:
  - Clock source: $I2CCLK = PCLK1 = 170\,\text{MHz}$.
  - Timing prescaler: `PRESC = 16` $\implies t_{PRESC} = (16 + 1) / 170\,\text{MHz} = 100\,\text{ns}$ ($10\,\text{MHz}$ kernel).
  - Target: $100\,\text{kHz}$ standard mode ($10\,\mu\text{s}$ period):
    - `SCLDEL = 4` ($500\,\text{ns}$ setup time), `SDADEL = 2` ($200\,\text{ns}$ hold time).
    - `SCLH = 49` $\implies t_{SCLH} = (49 + 1) \times 100\,\text{ns} = 5.0\,\mu\text{s}$.
    - `SCLL = 49` $\implies t_{SCLL} = (49 + 1) \times 100\,\text{ns} = 5.0\,\mu\text{s}$.
  - Resulting $t_{SCL} = 5.0\,\mu\text{s} + 5.0\,\mu\text{s} = 10.0\,\mu\text{s}$ ($100\,\text{kHz}$).

---

#### 5. Power Budget Analysis & LDO Evaluation
- **Current Demand on the 3.3 V Supply**:
  - ESP32 active current alone is approximately $240\,\text{mA}$ typical during Wi-Fi operations, with active transmit bursts demanding substantially higher peaks.
  - The pressure sensor requires $1\,\text{mA}$.
  - The STM32G474 running at $170\,\text{MHz}$ requires additional active current.
  - Even without accounting for peak RF bursts, the known base consumption is $240\,\text{mA} + 1\,\text{mA} = 241\,\text{mA}$, which leaves only $250\,\text{mA} - 241\,\text{mA} = 9\,\text{mA}$ of headroom from the $250\,\text{mA}$ rating.
- **Adequacy Assessment**:
  - The $250\,\text{mA}$ LDO is **inadequate**. It cannot safely power the STM32 running at $170\,\text{MHz}$ alongside the ESP32 transmitter and will suffer thermal throttling or output voltage brownouts during Wi-Fi transmission.
  - A higher-capacity voltage regulator is required.

---

### Bare-Metal C Implementation

```c
#include "stm32g4xx.h"

static void SystemClock_Config(void) {
    // 1. Enable Power Controller clock and configure Voltage Range 1 Boost Mode
    RCC->APB1ENR1 |= RCC_APB1ENR1_PWREN;

    // Set VOS to Range 1 (01b in PWR_CR1)
    PWR->CR1 = (PWR->CR1 & ~PWR_CR1_VOS_Msk) | (1UL << PWR_CR1_VOS_Pos);

    // Enter Boost Mode by clearing R1MODE in PWR_CR5
    PWR->CR5 &= ~PWR_CR5_R1MODE;

    // 2. Set Flash Latency to 4 WS for 170 MHz operation in Range 1 Boost Mode
    FLASH->ACR = (FLASH->ACR & ~FLASH_ACR_LATENCY_Msk) | FLASH_ACR_LATENCY_4WS;
    while ((FLASH->ACR & FLASH_ACR_LATENCY_Msk) != FLASH_ACR_LATENCY_4WS);

    // 3. Enable HSE (12 MHz crystal) and wait for ready
    RCC->CR |= RCC_CR_HSEON;
    while (!(RCC->CR & RCC_CR_HSERDY));

    // 4. Configure Main PLL: HSE (12 MHz) / 3 * 85 / 2 = 170 MHz
    // PLLM = 3 (encoded as 3 - 1 = 2)
    // PLLN = 85
    // PLLR = 2 (encoded as 00b)
    RCC->PLLCFGR = RCC_PLLCFGR_PLLSRC_HSE |
                   ((3UL - 1UL) << RCC_PLLCFGR_PLLM_Pos) |
                   (85UL << RCC_PLLCFGR_PLLN_Pos) |
                   (0UL << RCC_PLLCFGR_PLLR_Pos) |
                   RCC_PLLCFGR_PLLREN;

    // Enable PLL and wait until locked
    RCC->CR |= RCC_CR_PLLON;
    while (!(RCC->CR & RCC_CR_PLLRDY));

    // 5. Select PLL as system clock source (SYSCLK = 170 MHz, HCLK/PCLK1/PCLK2 = 170 MHz)
    RCC->CFGR = (RCC->CFGR & ~RCC_CFGR_SW_Msk) | RCC_CFGR_SW_PLL;
    while ((RCC->CFGR & RCC_CFGR_SWS_Msk) != RCC_CFGR_SWS_PLL);
}

static void Peripherals_Init(void) {
    // Enable GPIOA, GPIOB, USART2, and I2C1 peripheral clocks
    RCC->AHB2ENR |= RCC_AHB2ENR_GPIOAEN | RCC_AHB2ENR_GPIOBEN;
    RCC->APB1ENR1 |= RCC_APB1ENR1_USART2EN | RCC_APB1ENR1_I2C1EN;

    // ----------------------------------------------------
    // GPIO Configuration
    // PA2: USART2_TX (AF7, Push-Pull, High Speed)
    // PA3: USART2_RX (AF7, Floating Input)
    // ----------------------------------------------------
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(GPIO_MODER_MODE2_Msk | GPIO_MODER_MODE3_Msk);
    GPIOA->MODER |= (2UL << GPIO_MODER_MODE2_Pos) | (2UL << GPIO_MODER_MODE3_Pos); // AF mode (10b)

    GPIOA->AFR[0] &= ~(GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL2_Msk | GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL3_Msk);
    GPIOA->AFR[0] |= (7UL << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL2_Pos) | (7UL << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL3_Pos); // AF7

    GPIOA->OSPEEDR |= (3UL << GPIO_OSPEEDR_OSPEED2_Pos) | (3UL << GPIO_OSPEEDR_OSPEED3_Pos);

    // ----------------------------------------------------
    // PB8: I2C1_SCL (AF4, Open-Drain)
    // PB9: I2C1_SDA (AF4, Open-Drain)
    // ----------------------------------------------------
    GPIOB->MODER &= ~(GPIO_MODER_MODE8_Msk | GPIO_MODER_MODE9_Msk);
    GPIOB->MODER |= (2UL << GPIO_MODER_MODE8_Pos) | (2UL << GPIO_MODER_MODE9_Pos); // AF mode (10b)

    GPIOB->OTYPER |= GPIO_OTYPER_OT8 | GPIO_OTYPER_OT9; // Open Drain (1b)

    GPIOB->AFR[1] &= ~(GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL8_Msk | GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL9_Msk);
    GPIOB->AFR[1] |= (4UL << GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL8_Pos) | (4UL << GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL9_Pos); // AF4

    // ----------------------------------------------------
    // USART2 Configuration: 921600 baud, 8N1, Oversampling by 16
    // BRR = 170000000 / 921600 = 184 -> 923913 baud (0.034% error)
    // ----------------------------------------------------
    USART2->CR1 = 0;
    USART2->BRR = 184UL;
    USART2->CR1 |= USART_CR1_TE | USART_CR1_RE | USART_CR1_UE;

    // ----------------------------------------------------
    // I2C1 Configuration: 100 kHz Standard Mode @ 170 MHz PCLK1
    // PRESC = 16 (t_presc = 100 ns)
    // SCLDEL = 4 (500 ns setup time), SDADEL = 2 (200 ns hold time)
    // SCLH = 49 (5.0 us high), SCLL = 49 (5.0 us low)
    // ----------------------------------------------------
    I2C1->CR1 &= ~I2C_CR1_PE;
    I2C1->TIMINGR = (16UL << I2C_TIMINGR_PRESC_Pos) |
                    (4UL  << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLDEL_Pos) |
                    (2UL  << I2C_TIMINGR_SDADEL_Pos) |
                    (49UL << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLH_Pos)   |
                    (49UL << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLL_Pos);
    I2C1->CR1 |= I2C_CR1_PE;
}

int main(void) {
    SystemClock_Config();
    Peripherals_Init();

    while (1) {
        // Polling loop: read pressure sensor over I2C1 and transmit to ESP32 over USART2
    }
}
